<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tunis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tunis is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, located near the site of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, and historically an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> port. In the medieval and early modern periods it was a northern terminus of trans-Saharan trade. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> note that Sokoto's commercial network was connected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> and Tunis via the desert routes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tunis.jpg" alt="Tunis" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: T A · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minaret_et_patio_de_la_mosqu%C3%A9e_Zitouna_au_centre_de_la_M%C3%A9dina_de_Tunis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its towns, preserved at sites such as Dougga, El Djem, Sbeitla and Leptis Magna, are among the most complete Roman urban landscapes, and its mosaics fill the Bardo National Museum in <b>Tunis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The jund rose in 824 under Mansur ibn Nasr al-Tunbudhi, who held a fortress near <b>Tunis</b>; by September the rebels had occupied Tunis and Kairouan, and after Mansur was killed Amir ibn Nafi' inflicted a severe defeat on Ziyadat Allah I. The emir prevailed only in 827, with…</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles V’s armies defeated and captured Francis I of France at the Battle of Pavia in 1525, sacked Rome in 1527, took <b>Tunis</b> in 1535 and crushed the German Protestant princes at Mühlberg in 1547.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1257 an embassy from the king of Kanem, near Lake Chad, crossed the Sahara and presented the Hafsid ruler al-Mustansir with a giraffe, which caused a sensation in <b>Tunis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1348 Abu al-Hasan, who had lost Tunisia to the Arab revolt, was deposed by his son Abu Inan Faris, and the Black Death and the rebellions of Tlemcen and <b>Tunis</b> marked the turn; in 1358 Abu Inan was strangled by his own vizier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Uluj Ali, a Calabrian convert, became beylerbey in 1568, captured <b>Tunis</b> in 1569 and commanded the Ottoman left wing at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Military</a></li></ul></section>
